        "content": "In the official **Kurose & Ross 9th Edition Lecture Slides (Slides 7-49..67)**, the progression of concepts moves systematically from problem formulation to concrete Internet protocol design. Key lecture subtopics in this sequence include **Recall: MAC protocols, FDM: Frequency Division Multiplexing, OFDM: Orthogonal FDM, OFDM: Subcarriers, OFDMA: subcarriers, time**, illustrating how theoretical networking principles translate directly into deployed IETF RFCs and IEEE standards.\n\n![7.3 Wireless MAC: FDM, OFDM Orthogonal Subcarriers, OFDMA Resource Blocks & 802.11 CSMA/CA (RTS/CTS) — Kurose & Ross 9e Slide Figure](/images/networking/slides/ch7_image80.png)\n\nThe following synthesized lecture takeaways capture the essential protocol rules and design trade-offs from the slides:\n\n- **WiFi; satellite; control plane:** 4/5G, Bluetooth; (and many wired technologies)\n- **FDM: Frequency Division Multiplexing:** Separated by “guard bands” to avoid interference.\n- **FDM: Frequency Division Multiplexing:** Each subchannel has lower bit transmission rate than wideband channel: making multipath easier to accommodate; lower rate implementations easier.\n- **FDM: Frequency Division Multiplexing:** Transmissions can happen on different subchannels at same time (in parallel)\n- **Orthogonal:** Carefully designed, modulated so crosstalk among subcarriers cancels at center of each subcarrier frequency.\n- **OFDM: Subcarriers:** # subcarriers in an 4G / 5G channel depends on channel’s bandwidth.\n- **Chapter 7 Concept:** One “symbol” can be transmitted per 66.6 usec (4G) at given subcarrier frequency.\n- **Chapter 7 Concept:** Number of bits (between 2-8) in symbol depends on modulation technique chosen (which depends on channel quality)\n- **Resource block (RB):** Symbols grouped into resource blocks.\n- **OFDMA: resource blocks:** 84 symbols (7 symbols/slot over 12 subcarriers) in 4G."
