        "content": "To analyze **7.4C 5G Core Network Architecture: AMF, SMF, UPF, UDM, Control/User-Plane Separation (CUPS) & GTP-U** quantitatively, computer networking relies on precise mathematical models, bit-level header specifications, and deterministic state-update equations. The Governing Equation and Architectural Reference Table below summarize the core relationships for **Chapter 7 (Wireless & Mobile Networks (MIMO, QAM, OFDMA, 802.11 WiFi, 5G RAN/Core & Mobility))**:\n\n$$C = B \\log_2(1 + \\text{SNR})\\text{ bps}, \\qquad T_{\\text{CSMA/CA}} = \\text{DIFS} + T_{\\text{backoff}} + T_{\\text{DATA}} + \\text{SIFS} + T_{\\text{ACK}}$$\n\n| Wireless / 5G Layer | Physical / Protocol Mechanism | Operational Role in WiFi (802.11) & 5G Cellular Networks |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **RF Channel & MIMO** | Path loss, multipath fading, SNR (dB), $N_t \\times N_r$ antennas | Uses spatial diversity (reliability), spatial multiplexing (parallel streams), and beamforming to approach Shannon capacity |\n| **Adaptive Modulation (QAM)** | BPSK (1 b/sym), QPSK (2 b/sym), 16-QAM (4 b/sym), 64-QAM (6 b/sym) | Dynamically adapts Modulation and Coding Scheme (MCS) based on real-time channel SNR and Bit Error Rate (BER) |\n| **OFDMA Subcarriers** | 2D time-frequency grid of orthogonal subcarriers (Resource Blocks) | Eliminates inter-symbol interference (ISI) and schedules multi-user transmissions in 802.11ax (WiFi 6) and 5G NR |\n| **802.11 CSMA/CA & 4-Addr** | `DIFS` + random backoff + `SIFS` + `ACK` (`RTS/CTS`); `Addr1`–`Addr4` | Avoids collisions when simultaneous TX/RX collision detection is impossible; `Addr1` (RA), `Addr2` (TA), `Addr3` (Router MAC) |\n| **5G RAN & Core (CUPS)** | `SDAP -> PDCP -> RLC -> MAC -> PHY`; `AMF`, `SMF`, `UPF`, `UDM` | Separates Control Plane (`AMF`/`SMF`) from User Plane (`UPF`) with `GTP-U` IP tunnels and Split/SD-RAN (`RU`/`DU`/`CU` + `RIC`) |\n| **Mobility & Handover** | BSS roaming (WiFi) & Source-to-Target `gNB` Xn handover (5G) | Buffers in-flight packets and switches `UPF` `GTP-U` tunnel endpoints seamlessly as mobile devices move across cells |"
